WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.200
Login is an event, but session management is an ongoing state.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:12.660
After authentication succeeds, the system usually creates a session cookie, token, or other credential that represents the user or client for later requests.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.640
Protecting that state is just as important as protecting the login form.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.900
A valid session can continue representing the user long after the initial authentication event.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.160
Session cookies and bearer tokens are common mechanisms.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:31.700
A bearer token, at a high level, usually grants access to whoever presents it, within its validity and scope.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.320
That means token exposure can matter even if the original password remains safe.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:45.820
Refresh tokens can extend access by obtaining new access tokens, so their storage, rotation, revocation, and lifetime deserve special care.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:51.560
Token lifetime affects the window during which a token can be used before expiration or renewal is required.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:55.300
Very long lifetimes may increase exposure if a token is lost.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.720
Very short lifetimes may create usability and reliability problems if renewal is brittle.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:10.620
Revocation, logout behavior, device trust at a high level, and secure cookie attributes such as secure transport and script access restrictions all shape the risk profile.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.360
Authentication systems must protect what happens after login.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:22.340
Session fixation concepts at a high level involve an attacker influencing or reusing a session identifier across authentication.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.320
Logout should end meaningful access where the design expects it.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:33.740
Role changes, password resets, MFA resets, and account deactivation may need to affect existing sessions.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.600
Session design turns one-time authentication into ongoing access control reality.
